{:paths ["src"]
 ;; This said "src has no dependencies at all — this is the bottom of the
 ;; stack ... verified by grep at the 2026-08-05 split", and it stopped being
 ;; true when `kotobase/protocols/blocks.cljc` arrived after that split:
 ;;
 ;;   (:require [kotobase.store :as st])   ; -get -put -list -append, IStore
 ;;
 ;; Measured 2026-09-06: three surface repos (protocol-ipfs, -atproto,
 ;; -pinning) require `kotobase.protocols.blocks` from src and therefore need
 ;; `kotobase` on the classpath at runtime, with nothing saying so. A new
 ;; consumer discovers it as `Could not find namespace: kotobase.store` partway
 ;; through its first run, which is how this was found.
 ;;
 ;; ------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; It cannot be removed. Measured 2026-09-06, not deferred.
 ;;
 ;; The previous note here said removal "belongs in its own change", which
 ;; read as deferred-but-doable. It was attempted in that change and refused,
 ;; for three reasons, each measured on both runtimes:
 ;;
 ;; 1. Under nbb/SCI — the first-class runtime — there is NO path to an
 ;;    IStore's methods except the protocol var. A LocalStore instance's own
 ;;    properties are SCI internals (Rf/type/qj/Yb/G/K) and its prototype
 ;;    carries only the field name `state`. `-get`/`-put`/`-list`/`-append`
 ;;    are not reachable by interop, so `doc-port` cannot be built without
 ;;    this dependency however the shapes are told apart. (On the JVM a
 ;;    reflective `_get` does work on the inline deftype — runtime-specific,
 ;;    and it breaks for the extend-type case in 3 below.)
 ;;
 ;; 2. `satisfies?` is not the only line that needs it. `audit!` calls
 ;;    `st/-append`, and does so on a ctx that HAS a `:blocks` port — so the
 ;;    dependency is on every path carrying a `:store`, not just the legacy
 ;;    fallback. Control: `audit-needs-the-protocol-even-when-a-blocks-port-
 ;;    is-carried`.
 ;;
 ;; 3. No structural predicate can stand in for `satisfies?`. An IStore
 ;;    reached by `extend-type` over a `defrecord` is a working, supported
 ;;    bare store that reports `map?` true, `record?` true, `:blocks` nil and
 ;;    `:store` nil — so `map?` routing sends it to `(doc-port nil)`.
 ;;    Control: `a-record-shaped-istore-is-a-supported-shape-no-structural-
 ;;    test-can-see`.
 ;;
 ;; That third control is the one that earns its place. The duck-typing
 ;; change was applied — `(satisfies? st/IStore target)` -> `(not (map?
 ;; target))` — and the whole pre-existing 24-test suite stayed GREEN, because
 ;; every fixture in it is `local/local-store`, a deftype, for which the
 ;; stand-in happens to agree. The change would have shipped green and broken
 ;; a record-shaped store silently in production. The claim is now checked
 ;; rather than asserted, so it cannot rot a third time:
 ;;
 ;;   kbb --backend sci --classpath "src:test:<kotobase>/src" bin/run_tests.cljk
 ;;   kbb -M:test
 ;;
 ;; Removing this dependency needs a real design change (a block/document
 ;; port supplied by the shell on every path, or the protocol moved below
 ;; this repo), not a duck-type. Until then src/ is NOT the bottom of the
 ;; stack, and this is what says so.
 :deps {
        io.github.kotoba-lang/test {:local/root "../kotoba.test"}
        io.github.kotoba-lang/text {:local/root "../kotoba.text"}
        io.github.kotoba-lang/kotobase
        {:git/sha "bbf57df9dde5c1869182012860129a598382e088"}}
 ;; First-class runtime is nbb/cljs (repo-wide runtime priority):
 ;;   kbb --backend sci --classpath "src:test:<kotobase>/src" bin/run_tests.cljk
 :aliases
 {:test {:extra-paths ["test"]}}}
